  "worked_solution": "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of cured patients within a week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=50$ patients in the sample\n\n- the probability that the new drug has cured a patient within a week is $p=64%=0.64$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(50,0.64).$ \n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of cured patients within a week is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(1-0.64)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(50)(0.64)(0.36)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11.52} \\\\  & ≈3.394\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
